Article Recommendations
CONSPECTUS: Molecular recognition is one of the fundamental events in biological systems, as typified by enzymes that enable highly efficient and selective catalytic reactions through precise recognition of substrate(s) and cofactor(s) in the binding pockets. Chemists therefore have long been inspired by such excellent molecular systems to develop various synthetic receptors with well-defined binding sites. Their effort is currently being devoted to the construction of not only molecular receptors but also self-assembled host compounds possessing connected cavities (pores) in the crystalline frameworks to rationally design functional porous materials capable of efficiently adsorbing molecules or ions at binding sites on the pore walls. However, it
is still challenging to design multiple distinct binding sites that are precisely arranged in an identical framework, which is currently one of the most important targets in this field to realize elaborate molecular systems beyond natural enzymes.
In this Account, we provide an overview of porous crystals with well-defined molecular recognition sites. We first show several strategies for arranging macrocyclic binding sites in crystalline frameworks such as metal−organic frameworks, porous molecular crystals, and covalent organic frameworks. Porous metal−macrocycle frameworks (MMFs) that we have recently developed are then described as a new type of porous crystals with well-defined multiple distinct binding sites. The MMF-1 crystal, which was developed first and is composed of four stereoisomers of helical \(\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}\) −macrocycle complexes, has one-dimensional channels with dimensions of \(1.4\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.9\ \mathrm{nm}\) equipped with enantiomeric pairs of five distinct binding sites. This structural feature of MMF-1 therefore allows for site-selective and asymmetric arrangement of not only single but also multiple guest molecules in the crystalline channels based on molecular recognition between the guests and the multiple binding sites. This characteristic was also exploited to develop a heterogeneous catalyst by non-covalently immobilizing an organic acid on the pore surface of MMF-1 to conduct size-specific catalytic reactions. In addition, adsorption of a photoreactive substrate in MMF was found to switch the photoreaction pathway to cause another reaction with the aid of photoactivated \(\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers arranged on the pore walls. Furthermore, the dynamic, transient process of molecular arrangement incorporated in MMF-1 has been successfully visualized by single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis. The formation of homochiral MMF-2 composed of only \((P)\) - or \((M)\) -helical \(\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{3}\) −macrocycle complexes is also described. Thus, macrocycle-based porous crystals with a complex structure such as MMFs are expected to serve as novel porous materials that have great potential to mimic or surpass enzymes by utilizing well-defined multiple binding sites capable of spatially arranging a catalyst, substrate, and effector for highly selective and allosterically tunable catalytic reactions, which can be also visualized by crystallographic analysis because of their crystalline nature.
## INTRODUCTION
Molecular recognition, which takes place through non-covalent interactions among two or more molecules and/or ions, is a key event in biological processes such as transcription based on the complementary pairing of DNA/RNA, protein−protein interactions, enzymatic reactions, and drug−receptor interactions. For instance, enzymes recognize substrate(s) and cofactor(s) at predetermined multiple binding sites to spatially arrange and orient them in the same reaction space in order to realize extremely efficient and selective catalytic reactions under ambient conditions. This elaborate system is therefore one of the ultimate models for chemists in seeking a
supramolecular way to construct highly functional molecular systems.1 An initial pioneering approach along this line was molecular recognition based on cyclic oligosaccharides named cyclodextrins,2 which were first isolated from starch by Villiers in 1891. Cyclodextrins or their derivatives have been applied to several catalytic reactions,3 as they have an ability to recognize
various molecules in the hydrophobic cavity. Chemists were also motivated to develop various macrocyclic receptors such as cucurbiturils,4 calixarenes,1 cyclotriveratrylenes,5 and crown ethers,6 and this trend has been ongoing, as typified by the remarkable example of pillararenes first reported by Ogoshi and co-workers in 2008.7 In addition to organic receptors, a large class of metal−organic supramolecular receptors have long shown promise. For instance, Fujita and co-workers have synthesized many kinds of coordination cages, capsules, tubes, and spheres based on metal coordination between a \(\mathrm{Pd^{II}}\) ion and a pyridyl moiety.8 Moreover, the large and threedimensional (3D) binding cavities of such metal−organic receptors enable them to catalyze space-specific and sometimes unusual reactions through encapsulation of substrate(s) and/or catalyst(s).9,10
Attention in molecular recognition is now expanding from discrete molecular receptors to polymeric host frameworks, especially porous crystals such as zeolites, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), and molecular crystals. For example, MOFs composed of metal nodes and organic linkers possess connected cavities (pores) in the crystals, and the pores are capable of accommodating molecules postsynthetically to demonstrate space functions such as separation,11 storage,12 transportation,13 catalysis,14 and structural determination.15 To drastically improve their functions, guest species are required to be arranged precisely in the pore on the basis of distinct molecular recognition modes with the host framework to induce host−guest and guest−guest interactions in a synergetic manner. Therefore, equipping crystalline pore walls with effective binding sites is a key target.16−18 Moreover, if not single but multiple binding sites were precisely provided in a specific pore (Figure 1), it would be possible to realize
elaborate functions comparable to or even different from enzymatic functions. Thus, the de novo design of such structures using only artificial molecules is currently a quite challenging task.
This Account provides an overview of effective strategies for equipping crystalline pore walls with binding sites to diversely functionalize porous crystals. Furthermore, as one of the emerging examples of porous crystals with well-defined multiple binding sites, we present a porous molecular crystal that we have recently developed, called a metal−macrocycle framework (MMF). Moreover, its unique functions based on molecular recognition and arrangement on the pore surfaces of nanochannels equipped with five sets of different enantiomerically paired binding sites are also discussed.
## STRATEGIES TO EQUIP CRYSTALLINE PORE WALLS WITH BINDING SITES
To introduce molecular binding sites into porous crystals, several types of approaches have been demonstrated to date.
For instance, MOF structures have been chemically modified by attaching functional groups to their organic ligands. In 2002, Yaghi and co-workers reported isoreticular MOFs, which are composed of organic ligands modified with several functional groups such as bromo, amino, and alkyl groups.19 Moreover, this approach has been further developed to upgrade their pores with distinct molecular binding sites. For instance, ligands tethering a typical receptor compound such as a crown ether20,21 or pillar[5]arene22 (Figure 2a) as the side chain have been utilized to construct MOF structures so that their molecular binding sites in the MOFs could recognize receptor-specific guest compounds in the crystalline states (Figure 2b).
As another approach, receptor compounds themselves can also be used as the organic ligands of MOFs (Figure 2c). For instance, Stoddart and co-workers used \(\gamma\) -cyclodextrin (γ-CD) as an organic ligand to construct CD-MOFs with alkali ions (Figure 3).23 They also demonstrated that CD-MOFs can accommodate several guest compounds such as dyes,23 carbon dioxide,24 and ibuprofen25 in the porous crystals. In addition, liquid-phase chromatographic separation of a BTEX (benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylene isomers) mixture using CDMOFs as the stationary phase was achieved through the molecular recognition ability of the \(\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}\) cavity.26 Cyclotriveratrylene (CTV) is another candidate for use in constructing MOF-like structures with alkali ions because its veratrole moieties can act as bidentate ligands to bind alkali ions (Figure 4a,b). Raston and Hardie reported coordination polymers composed of CTV, alkali ions, and cobalt(III) bis(dicarbollide) or halogenated monocarbaborane as their counteranion.27,28 Our group also reported a CTV-based coordination polymer with an \(\alpha\) -Keggin-type polyoxometalate, \(\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD) of this polymnere revealed that the \(\mathrm{PMo}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}{}^{3-}\) anions are arranged in the network structure of \([\mathrm{Na}^{+}{}_{3}(\mathrm{CTV})_{2}]_{n}\) .29 It was also found that in the crystalline state the CTV cavity in the coordination polymer functions as a molecular binding site. When an appropriate guest such as 1,4-benzoquinone was added to the crystallization solution, the guest molecules were efficiently encapsulated in the CTV cavities through \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions, as proven by single-crystal XRD (Figure 4c).
Not only MOFs but also molecular crystals can provide pores capable of binding or transporting molecules or ions.30,31 The construction of molecular crystals composed of macrocyclic receptors is also a facile way to provide molecular binding sites in the crystal (Figure 2d). For instance, Seo, Kim, and co-workers reported that cucurbit[6]uril (Figure 2a) recrystallized from a concentrated HCl solution formed a stable porous crystal structure that was maintained even after activation at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a vacuum for 2 days. In addition, the porous crystal adsorbed several guest compounds such as acetylene and carbon dioxide in the cavity of cucurbit[6]uril and the interstitial 1D channels in its honeycomb-like packing structure.32,33 Another typical class of macrocyclic receptors, calix[4]arene (Figure 2a) and its derivatives, were also found to form molecular crystals capable of binding or transporting small molecules.34,35 In addition, several new macrocyclic compounds such as dehydrobenzannulene derivatives and oligoamine, -imine, and -urea macrocycles have recently been utilized as building blocks of molecular crystals with intrinsic porosity.36−39 This strategy has also been applied to recently developed organic cages.40,41 For instance, an organic cage synthesized by the condensation reaction of 1,3,5-triformyl
benzene and 1,2-diaminocyclohexane (Figure 2a) allows the arrangement of guest species such as \(\mathrm{I}_{5}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{OsO}_{4}\) in the porous crystal, as observed by single-crystal XRD analysis. The cage cavity of the porous crystal was also utilized to separate mesitylene from its structural isomers.
It should be noted that several macrocycles or cages can also be connected with other organic linkers through covalent bonds to construct covalent organic frameworks, which have recently attracted much attention as new crystalline porous materials with unique catalytic or luminescence properties as well as gas adsorption properties.
## ## CRYSTALLINE PORE WALLS MODIFIED WITH MULTIPLE FUNCTIONAL GROUPS
As described in the previous section, arranging molecular binding sites on the inner pore surfaces of the crystal is an excellent way to recognize or arrange neutral molecules, ions, and ionic species in the crystalline state. To further bring out the potential of such porous crystals, it should be one of the promising ways to provide multiple binding sites in an identical pore. In MOFs, Yaghi and co-workers developed multivariate MOFs (MTV-MOFs) in which up to eight kinds of functional groups were introduced into the identical crystal of a terephthalate-based MOF (Figure 5). Interestingly, some MTV-MOFs showed better selectivity for the uptake of carbon dioxide over carbon monoxide due to the complex arrangement of several functional groups in the pore. However, it was difficult to control the arrangement of the functional groups because of their random distribution in the structure of MTV-MOFs. Therefore, one of the next targets of this area is designing porous crystals with precisely arranged multiple functionalities or binding sites within an identical pore.
## ## METAL−MACROCYCLE FRAMEWORK WITH MULTIPLE BINDING POCKETS IN ITS SINGLE NANOCHANNEL
In 2012, our group fortuitously obtained a porous crystal possessing crystalline pore walls precisely decorated with multiple binding sites, and we named it metal−macrocycle framework-1 (MMF-1). MMF-1 is composed of metal−macrocycle complexes \(\left[\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{LCl}_{6}\right]\) synthesized from the tris( \(\alpha\)-phenylenediamine)-based macrocyclic hexaamine ligand L and 3 equiv of \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\). Interestingly, this
metal complexation gives four stereoisomers of helical \(\left[\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{LCl}_{6}\right]\): \((P)\)-syn, \((M)\)-syn, \((P)\)-anti, and \((M)\)-anti isomers (Figure 6a). The \((P)\) and \((M)\) isomers are derived from their helical sense, and the syn and anti isomers are caused by a conformational difference in \(\mathbf{L}\) that possesses three \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\) centers on one side \((syn)\) or one on one side and two on the other side (anti) of twisted \(\mathbf{L}\). Each isomer has a two-faced pocket, with one face providing a polar binding pocket with more \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\) centers (head pocket) and the other face being more hydrophobic because it has more phenylene rings (tail pocket) (Figure 6b,c). An equimolar amount of four isomers \((P)\)-syn, \((M)\)-syn, \((P)\)-anti, and \((M)\)-anti) cocrystallizes in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) to form yellow plate-shaped porous crystals of MMF-1 through intermolecular hydrogen bonding between \(\mathrm{Pd-N-H}\) and \(\mathrm{Cl-Pd}\) moieties as well as \(\mathrm{Pd-Pd}\) interactions. As the result, the \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-syn isomers form 2D sheets (syn sheets) along the \(a\) and \(c\) axes, and the syn sheets are bridged by one-dimensional tapes (anti tapes) formed by \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-anti isomers along the \(c\) axis to construct nanochannels along the \(c\) axis with dimensions of \(1.4\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.9\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 6d).
Notably, the combination of syn sheets and anti tapes gives rise to multiple binding sites in the single nanochannel of MMF-1. For instance, the ceiling of the channel is equipped with \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-anti head pockets, and the floor is
composed of \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-anti tail pockets. On the other hand, \((P)\)- or \((M)\)-syn tail pockets are exposed on opposite lateral walls, respectively (Figure 6d; pocket 2). As a result, the channel surrounded by the multiple pockets is divided into two enantiomeric sides \((P)\) and \((M)\) sides) across a glide plane at the center of the channel. In addition to the three enantiomeric binding pockets (anti head, anti tail, and syn tail pockets), there are two other enantiomeric interstitial voids at the bottom corners of the channel. One is an elliptical void with some hydrogen-bonding sites such as \(\mathrm{N-H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pd-Cl}\) (Figure 6d; void 4), and the other is a tubular void accommodating one acetonitrile molecule that comes from the crystallization solvent (Figure 6d; void 5). The channel is therefore equipped with five enantiomeric binding sites (three pockets and two voids). Although several porous crystals composed of other metal−macrocycle complexes had also been reported at that time, to the best of our knowledge, a complex pore structure with multiple binding pockets as shown in MMF-1 had not been constructed previously. This indicates that a key to the formation of an elaborate MMF structure is the simultaneous formation and following cocrystallization of four helical isomers owing to the appropriate flexibility of the metal−macrocycle complexes. In contrast, Yamagishi and Aida recently reported the construction of a porous molecular crystal with high structural complexity comparable to MMF-1
by using a single molecule with a propeller shape,49 which indicates that the de novo design of such complex crystal structures is still difficult.
This unique channel structure of MMF-1 with well-defined multiple binding sites therefore prompted us to demonstrate site-selective arrangement of several guest molecules in the channel. The experimental procedure was simple: MMF-1 crystals were soaked in a guest solution at room temperature for several hours to a few days, and then one of the crystals was analyzed by single-crystal XRD to determine the molecular structure(s) arranged on the channel surface. As the MMF-1 crystal is stable and insoluble in several solvents such as \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\), alcohols, acetone, \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\), and acidic water, these solvents could be used for the soaking experiments without a significant loss of single-crystallinity. For instance, MMF-1 crystals were soaked in a \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) solution of benzene (ca. \(6\,\mathrm{M}\)) for 1 day, and the crystals were then analyzed by single-crystal XRD. The resulting crystal structure revealed that benzene molecules were site-selectively captured by the \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-anti head pockets at the ceiling and the \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-syn tail pockets at the lateral walls with occupancies of 82 and \(66\%\), respectively (Figure 7a). On the other hand, naphthalene and its isomer, azulene, were found to adsorb on the \((P)\)- and \((M)\)-syn tail pockets and the \((P)\) and \((M)\) elliptical voids at the bottom corners (Figure 7b,c). Naphthalene and azulene were arranged differently from benzene, suggesting that the binding
pockets on the channel surface provide an excellent platform to recognize the shapes of guest molecules, as is evident from the fact that azulene fits in exactly with its binding pocket (Figure 7c).47 In addition to the nonsubstituted aromatic compounds, site-selective arrangement of substituted benzene derivatives such as benzonitrile, anisole, acetophenone, nitrobenzene, and \(p\)-toluenesulfonic acid were also observed (Figure 7d).50 Furthermore, it was found that a chiral benzene derivative, (1S)- or (1R)-1-(3-chlorophenyl)ethanol, was asymmetrically arranged on the channel surface on the basis of diastereoselective recognition at each chiral binding site.47 For instance, (1S)-1-(3-chlorophenyl)ethanol molecules bound only to the \((P)\)-anti head and \((M)\)-syn tail pockets and the \((P)\) elliptical voids (Figure 7e), and the selectivity was supported by the significantly low value of its Flack parameter (0.13(3)). As expected, the arranged structure of its enantiomer, (1R)-1-(3-chlorophenyl)ethanol, was obtained in a mirror image arrangement of the (S) isomer. These results indicate that the MMF-1 channel can be asymmetrically decorated with an appropriate chiral guest to provide a potential space for chiral recognition and catalytic reactions.
## SIMULTANEOUS ARRANGEMENT OF TWO OR THREE GUEST MOLECULES IN MMF-1
The multiple binding sites of MMF-1 also allow for arrangements of two or three different molecules. For instance, meta and para isomers of dibromobenzene were treated with MMF-1 in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\), and the resulting crystal was analyzed by single-crystal XRD. The obtained crystal structure revealed that the meta isomer was site-selectively arranged on the \((P/M)\)-anti head and \((P/M)\)-syn tail pockets and the para isomer was observed on different binding sites, the \((P/M)\) elliptical voids (Figure 8a). It should be noted here that each isomer was arranged in the same manner as in the cases with single adsorption of the meta or para isomer. This result also demonstrates the shape-sorting capability of MMF-1 based on highly selective molecular recognition among guest molecules and binding sites.
Simultaneous arrangement of three molecules in MMF-1 was further achieved by the combination of tetrathiafulvalene, hydroxymethylferrocene, and 9-fluorenylmethanol as guests (Figure 8b). In the resulting ternary structure, tetrathiafulvalene was adsorbed on the \((P/M)\)-syn tail pockets through van der Waals contacts. In addition, the other two guests, hydroxymethylferrocene and 9-fluorenylmethanol, were site-selectively arranged on the ceiling and the \((P/M)\) elliptical voids, respectively, through hydrogen bonding between their hydroxymethyl groups and exposed \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pd}-\mathrm{Cl}\) moieties on the channel surface. Although the occupancies of the guests were estimated to range from 50 to \(60\%\) in the XRD analysis, the arrangement structure suggests the existence of several guest−guest interactions such as \(\mathrm{CHI}-\pi\) and \(\mathrm{S}-\pi\) interactions. To the best of our knowledge, site-selective coarrangement of three different compounds within a single pore has been rarely
reported. Moreover, this result can also be regarded as 1D cocrystallization of three molecules in the channels, which never occurs in common bulk systems.
## IN SITU X-RAY SNAPSHOT ANALYSIS OF A MOLECULAR ARRANGEMENT PROCESS
Besides the steady states of molecular arrangement discussed above, the observation of dynamic processes of molecular arrangement is also important to understand the functions of porous structures. We therefore designed an experiment to visualize transient states of the molecular arrangement process in MMF-1 by using (1R)-1-(3-chlorophenyl)ethanol as a guest. MMF-1 crystals were first soaked in acetonitrile solution of the guest for \( 4.5~\mathrm{min} \) at \( 23\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and then immediately cooled to \( -180\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) to measure single-crystal XRD to take the first snapshot. During the measurement, molecular arrangement was suspended because the temperature \( (-180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}) \) was significantly lower than the melting point of the acetonitrile solution. In the first snapshot, the guest molecules were observed on the \( (M) \)-anti head pockets and the
\( (M) \) elliptical voids, but the \( (P) \)-syn tail pockets were still occupied by an acetonitrile molecule. To advance the molecular arrangement, the crystal was warmed to \( -40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), kept for \( 30\ \mathrm{min} \), and then cooled to \( -180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) again to obtain the second X-ray snapshot. As a result, the guest appeared at interstices next to the \( (P) \)-syn tail pockets. In the third X-ray snapshot after further warming at \( -40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 1\ \mathrm{h} \), another guest molecule was additionally observed on the \( (P) \)-syn tail pockets, and some of the acetonitrile molecules in the pockets was released instead. Finally, the guest at the interstices disappeared and the arrangement structure settled down as the steady-state structure in the fourth X-ray snapshot taken after additional warming at \( -40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 3\ \mathrm{h} \) (Figure 9).
Considering the four X-ray snapshots, we found that the guest molecules were arranged in a stepwise manner via several transient states before reaching the steady state. In the transient states, the guest was kinetically trapped on tentative binding sites that are different from the final binding sites in the steady state. Our experimental design was indeed modeled on a previous study of the XRD observation of a reaction
intermediate formed in a porous coordination polymer,53 and this approach has been also applied to the direct observation of an organometallic reaction54 or metal cluster formation.55 However, visualization of non-covalent or supramolecular processes such as molecular adsorption and arrangement had been scarcely demonstrated by artificial porous crystals except for MMF-1.
## SIZE-SPECIFIC CATALYTIC REACTION IN MMF-1 NON-COVALENTLY IMMOBILIZING ACID MOLECULES
As another application of MMF-1, catalyst molecules were also immobilized in the MMF channels to use the resulting crystals as heterogeneous catalysts.56 For instance, MMF-1 crystals were treated with \(p\) -toluenesulfonic acid monohydrate ( \(\scriptstyle\left(p\right.\) - \(\mathrm{TsOH}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) to encapsulate \(p{\mathrm{-TsOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and then washed repeatedly with \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\) to remove the excess amount of \(p{\mathrm{-TsOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . Interestingly, a certain amount of \(p{\mathrm{-TsOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) stably remained in MMF-1 after several washes, as confirmed by \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR analysis after digestion of the crystals. This result implies that \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}\) , the countercation of \(p{\mathrm{-TsO}}^{-}\) , works as an anchor to immobilize \(p\) - \(\mathrm{TsO^{-}}\) on the channel surfaces through hydrogen bonding with exposed \(\mathrm{N-H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pd}{-}\mathrm{Cl}\) moieties of \([\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{LCl}_{6}]\) in a weakly polar environment of MMF-1 filled with \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\) .
We next examined acid-catalyzed deprotection of a trityl group using the resulting MMF-1 crystals with stably immobilized \(p{\mathrm{-TsOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules. When trityl-protected benzyl alcohol was used as a substrate, the deprotection reaction occurred slowly after incubation for several days. It seemed that the encapsulation of the substrate into the channels took considerable time because of the relatively large molecular size of the substrate ( \(1.1\ \mathrm{nm}\) kinetic diameter). In contrast, a porphyrin-based substrate with a kinetic diameter of \(1.7\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which is larger than the channel dimensions \(\left(1.4\:\mathrm{nm}\times\right.\) \(1.9\ \mathrm{nm}\rangle\) ), did not react at all even after 20 days (Figure 10). This size specificity indicates that the catalytic deprotection reaction takes place only in the channels. In addition, no acid leaching was detected in the filtrate after the deprotection reaction, suggesting that the MMF-1 crystals can stably immobilize the acid and indeed worked as a heterogeneous acid catalyst. Moreover, the MMF-1 catalyst could be
deactivated by washing away \(p{\mathrm{-TsOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) from the MMF-1 catalyst by soaking in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) . This reversibility is a sharp contrast to conventional MOF catalysts, where catalytic moieties are covalently immobilized on the frameworks.57
## PREFERENTIAL PHOTOREACTION IN MMF-1
We recently developed a preferential photoreaction in MMF-1 based on site-selective recognition of a substrate and photoactivation of \(\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers on the channel surface (Figure 11). For instance, a 1,6-diene, which normally forms an intramolecular \([2\ +\ 2]\) cycloadduct under photoirradiation, preferentially undergoes a photoinduced olefin migration in MMF-1, and the alternative \([2\ +\ 2]\) cycloaddition is completely inhibited in the confined space, probably due to a photoshielding effect by the crystalline framework and/or restriction of the conformational change of the 1,6-diene adsorbed on the channel surface. A plausible reaction mechanism that the olefin migration was triggered by photoinduced dissociation of the \(\mathrm{Pd}{-}\mathrm{Cl}\) bond was suggested on the basis of UV−vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy, single-crystal XRD, and MS-CASPT2 calculations. The substrate scope of the photoinduced olefin migration in MMF was also reported using substituted allylbenzene derivatives.58
## HOMOCHIRAL MMF-2
The introduction and control of chirality in porous crystals have attracted much attention for designing space-specific chiral functions such as asymmetric catalysis, chiral separation, and anisotropic transportation.59−63 However, MMF-1 composed of both \((P)\) - and \((M)\) -syn/anti isomers of \([\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{LCl}_{6}]\) is a racemic compound as described above. If the ratio of the isomers, especially \((P)\) and \((M)\) , were to be biased, a homochiral MMF would be obtained. Control of the isomer ratio was successfully achieved by changing the solvent for the complexation of \(\mathbf{L}\) and \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) to 9:1 \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}/\mathrm{DMSO}\) .63 After the reaction and subsequent crystallization, not plate- but tetrahedron-shaped yellow crystals were obtained. Single-crystal XRD analysis of the new crystal, named MMF-2, revealed that, unlike MMF-1, the crystal possessed not 1D but rather 3D complicated pores and, more importantly, that MMF-2 was composed of only syn isomers. However, the resulting MMF-2 crystal was a racemic solid
solution composed of equimolar amounts of \((P)\) - and \((M)\) - MMF-2 crystals, as proven by the Flack parameter \(\left(0.50(5)\right)\) in the XRD analysis.
To further bias the ratio of \((P)\) - and \((M)\) -syn isomers during the formation of MMF-2, a variety of chiral additives were added to the crystallization solution. For instance, when Dglucono-1,5-lactone was used as an additive, \((P)\) -enriched MMF-2 crystals were predominantly obtained, as confirmed by the significantly lower value of the Flack parameter (0.07(9)). In contrast, \((M)\) -enriched MMF-2 crystals were predominantly formed when another chiral reagent, D-glucurono-6,3-lactone, was used (Figure 12). In both cases, the resulting crystals did not include the chiral additives, as shown in the \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR analysis of solutions containing the digested crystals. This suggests that the chiral additives could play a crucial role in the initial asymmetric induction process through hydrogen bonding between carbonyl and hydroxyl groups of the additives and the \(\mathrm{N-H}\) and \(\mathrm{Pd-Cl}\) moieties of \(\left[\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{LCl}_{6}\right]\). This phenomenon is consistent with a previous report in which homochiral MOF-5 crystals were synthesized in the presence of \(\mathrm{L}-\) or D-proline as a chiral additive, which was also not included in the resulting crystals.64
## CONCLUSION
This Account describes several advanced approaches to provide porous molecular crystals with macrocyclic binding sites to develop their pore functions based on host−guest interactions and molecular arrangement. It is still a challenging task to design crystalline pore walls by arranging distinct binding pockets for molecules and functionalities in a welldefined manner. A number of self-assembled porous crystals reported to date clearly demonstrate that self-assembly of
molecular components capable of binding molecules or functionalities have great potential in the design of confined pore spaces. Supramolecular spaces created in such a way would provide a promising platform for molecular arrangement and transformation with high efficiency and selectivity. With this view, our group developed a porous molecular crystal, MMF-1, that possesses nanochannels equipped with five enantiomeric binding pockets and voids derived from four stereoisomers of helical metallomacrocycles \(\left[\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{LCl}_{6}\right]\). MMF-1 is capable of arranging many types of guest molecules site-selectively on the channel surfaces through non-covalent interactions between the guests and the multiple binding sites. This molecular recognition ability has been also applied to diastereoselective arrangement of chiral molecules, a size-specific heterogeneous acid catalyst, and simultaneous arrangement of two or three different molecules. Furthermore, the in situ XRD observation technique also enabled visualization of a stepwise molecular arrangement process in MMF-1 through some transient adsorption structures. These findings prompted us to design more elaborate systems directed toward artificial enzymes. For instance, asymmetric arrangements of acids, bases, or catalysts in MMF-1 should mimic catalytic centers in natural enzymes. Furthermore, just by the use of different solvents and chiral additives, homochiral porous crystals (MMF-2, etc.) with another type of porous structure can be obtained. Such porous crystals with well-defined multiple binding sites in the pores also have high potential for chiral separation and anisotropic transport based on the asymmetric modification of the pores. Thus, we believe that a series of MMF crystals can create a new category of functionalized porous materials.
