Shohei Tashiro is an Associate Professor in the Department of Chemistry of the Graduate School of Science at The University of Tokyo. He received his B.S. in 2001 and his M.S. in 2003 from Nagoya University. In 2005 he accepted a position as an Assistant Professor at The University of Tokyo before he received his Ph.D. from The University of Tokyo in 2006. In 2015 he was appointed to his current position. His current research interest is supramolecular chemistry.
Mitsuhiko Shionoya is a Professor in the Department of Chemistry of the Graduate School of Science at The University of Tokyo. He received his B.S in 1982 and M.S. in 1984 from The University of Tokyo. In 1986 he accepted a position as an Assistant Professor at Hiroshima University. From 1988 to 1990 he held a position as an Assistant Professor at the Institute for Molecular Science in Okazaki, Japan. He received his Ph.D. from Hiroshima University in 1990. He was appointed as a Lecturer in 1991 and then an Associate Professor in 1994 at Hiroshima University. In 1991 he was a JSPS Visiting Scientist at the University of Texas at Austin in the United State. In 1995 he was promoted to Professor at the Institute for Molecular Science. In 1999 he was appointed to his current position. His research interests are centered on the development of bioinspired supramolecular architectures such as artificial metallo-DNA and peptides, dynamic coordination capsules and cages, metal−macrocycle frameworks, molecular machines, and coordination asymmetry.
